WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.349
If you want to get a politician to

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.209
change the subject, ask them

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.319
how they're going to deal with the

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.449
federal debt, which is growing

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.179
to be bigger than all of the US GDP

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.099
and has us on a path to fiscal

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.149
disaster.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.619
That's what happened when I asked

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.599
GOP presidential candidate Vivek

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.639
Ramaswamy what he would do

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.469
to cut spending.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.179
He said he'd focus instead on

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.129
supercharging the economy

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.959
so that we can grow our way out

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:25.529
of it.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:26.549
It's as though we've forgotten

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.929
that's an option. And all of those

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:28.919
projections that say we're going to

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.749
run out of money, assume

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.158
you know fairly enough, I guess, the

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:33.569
current GDP growth rate.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:34.739
And if you extrapolate that out,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.609
yes, we're going bust economically,

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:37.769
you know, financially and fiscally

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.059
speaking. It doesn't have to be that

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.359
way.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.769
Our government has been on an

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.899
economic bender for this entire

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:43.979
century.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.349
And in 2023, the national

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.969
debt held by the public will be

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.039
roughly equal to our gross domestic

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.109
product, and the debt is projected

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.639
to get bigger and bigger over the

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.479
coming years.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.369
We already know the Boomer response

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.319
to questions about cutting spending,

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.149
especially entitlements.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.529
And I won't cut a single bit of

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.629
Medicare or Social Security.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.369
Under no circumstances should

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.259
Republicans vote to cut a

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.179
single penny from Medicare or

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.499
Social Security.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.789
So what happens when you bring up

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.790
cutting spending to Vivek

00:01:13.791 --> 00:01:15.059
Ramaswamy? He starts talking about

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.009
unleashing economic growth so guess

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:17.789
what?

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.529
We don't really have to tackle the

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.939
major drivers of debt and deficits.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.189
We've grown at more than four plus

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.539
percent GDP growth for most of our

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.679
national history.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.749
If we get back to a three handle,

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.729
three point something, actually

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.139
most of those fiscal problems are

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.279
completely gone.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.509
According to figures from the

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.159
nonpartisan Congressional Budget

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.039
Office or CBO, since 1970,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.259
annual GDP growth has averaged

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.209
2.7%, helped

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.009
along by a rapidly growing labor

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.419
force thanks to immigration, the

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.279
baby boom and women going

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.269
to work.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.519
So what happens if Ramaswamy becomes

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.439
president and is actually able to

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.359
unleash economic growth that

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.189
pushes the US economy to average

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.499
4% annual GDP growth

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.209
for two full terms?

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.099
Would most of our fiscal problems

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.079
be completely gone?

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.459
Not even close.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.289
The CBO already projects that

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.299
GDP will grow by 2% per

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.369
year through 2033.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.199
For argument's sake, let's assume

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.089
that GDP growth runs twice that at

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.159
4% a year, a rate

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.199
far above what it's been so far

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.999
in the 21st century.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.069
In a recent report, the CBO

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.869
estimated that for every 1.2

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.239
percentage points that GDP exceeds

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.579
its projected level for 2033,

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.489
the deficit would be $51

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:32.939
billion smaller.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.099
By that measure, if GDP growth

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.079
ran 4% instead of 2% over

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.909
those eight years, the projected

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.889
deficit in 2033

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.749
of $1.4 trillion

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.669
would be cut almost in half

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.799
to about $700 billion.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.899
In other words, we'd be better off,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.489
but the government would still be

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.319
running red ink every

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.299
year and the federal debt would

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.769
just keep growing.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.869
We can't let Ramaswamy or

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.699
anyone running for president duck

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.859
questions about cutting spending.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.569
These guys want to steer clear of

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.849
the third rail of American politics;

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.409
Social Security and Medicare

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.759
entitlements.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.649
But we have to make them grab

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.019
it fully with both hands.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.939
If we don't start seriously cutting

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.189
spending and scaling back elder

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.019
care entitlements, we're not going

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.729
to balance the budget or stop

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.229
growing the debt.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.119
And our nation's economy

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.559
is headed for disaster.

